     "notes": {
      "text": "1...c6, the Caro-Kann, prepares ...d5 so a pawn can recapture there. Compared with 1...e6 (French), your light-squared bishop stays free, but c6 is taken from your knight and a later ...c5 costs a move. 1...e5 and 1...c5 fight for d4 instead, while 1...f5? weakens your kingside.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory/1. e4",
       "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory/1._e4",
